{"id":"pltr-earnings-q3-2026","title":"PLTR earnings — Palantir, Q3 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-11-02","html":"<p>Management sees more demand than it can serve and raised every line of its outlook; the thing that could spoil it is that new contract signings have stopped growing as fast as revenue.</p>\n<p>Reported 2026-11-02. Next report: not found (early February 2027 is an outside estimate). Updated 2026-11-12. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> stronger. Revenue +96%; US commercial +148% and now larger than US government.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> raised on every line. 2026 revenue $8.530–8.534B (from $8.150–8.158B); adjusted operating income $5.245–5.249B (from $4.889–4.897B); adjusted free cash flow $4.8–5.0B (from $4.5–4.7B). Q4 revenue $2.649–2.653B.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> bookings. Contract value signed rose only 6% from a year ago, and remaining performance obligations were flat in the quarter at $4.9B.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> leans up, narrowly. Price sits on my 38.2% line at $172.23; a close below $159.66 (the 50% line) would flip it.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $2.31B (+96%) vs $2.18B estimate, and well above its own $2.160–2.164B guide.</li><li>EPS $0.44 (GAAP and adjusted are the same this quarter) vs $0.376 estimate. A clean beat on both.</li><li>Net dollar retention 165%; US commercial customers 686.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> revenue $2.649–2.653B. Nothing on 2027.</li><li><strong>Demand:</strong> ahead of what the company can serve, led by US commercial and a new &quot;Sovereign AI&quot; offering. &quot;We really cannot satiate demand.&quot; (Alex Karp, CEO; third-party transcript)</li><li><strong>Bookings:</strong> total contract value signed $2.92B, +6% from a year ago (it grew 61% in Q1 and 49% in Q2, per TIKR). Karp put the slowdown down to several large deals signed on shorter initial terms.</li><li><strong>Backlog:</strong> remaining deal value $13.4B (+2% in the quarter); remaining performance obligations $4.9B, flat.</li><li><strong>US commercial:</strong> 2026 revenue now above $3.559B (from above $3.424B).</li><li><strong>International commercial:</strong> the soft spot, +1% in the quarter.</li><li><strong>AI labs:</strong> described as widening Palantir's market; management was critical of software firms that resell AI usage as revenue.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Dependence on a new offering.</strong> The filing says growth depends on continued adoption of new products; &quot;Sovereign AI&quot; is carrying a large share of the story.</li><li><strong>Customer concentration.</strong> The top three customers are 16% of revenue.</li><li><strong>Contracts that can be ended for convenience.</strong> Standard in government work; it means backlog is softer than it looks.</li><li><strong>Government budget delays.</strong> US government revenue was $953M in the quarter.</li><li><strong>AI regulation.</strong></li><li><strong>Stock-based pay.</strong> $318.9M in the quarter, +85%.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-bookings-not-keeping-pace-both-ways\">The big worry: bookings not keeping pace, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Short-term deals renewing and growing. Net dollar retention of 165% says existing customers are spending far more each year.</li><li>Contract value signed re-accelerating in Q4. One quarter of 6% after 61% and 49% could be timing.</li><li>A defense budget that lands. The Army enterprise agreement runs 10 years and up to $10B, and a Pentagon memo directs up to $243.9M of no-bid Palantir spending through Mar 31, 2027 (Defense One).</li><li>Compute supply arriving. The AI builders you track all say demand exceeds supply, and Palantir's customers need that capacity to deploy.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>A second quarter of single-digit bookings growth while revenue guidance still assumes a near doubling.</li><li>A budget fight. The funding stopgap expires Dec 11, 2026, and about $350B of the $1.5T fiscal 2027 defense request depends on a bill the incoming Congress is expected to block (Defense One).</li><li>Short contracts not renewing. Shorter initial terms mean customers can leave sooner.</li><li>The price of the stock. TIKR puts it at 94 times forward earnings; at that price a flat backlog matters more than it would elsewhere.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>No debt, $10.4B in cash and Treasuries, capex about $30M in nine months. It does not need the credit markets, unlike the AI builders.</li><li>Growing 96% against ServiceNow's 20%; on the evidence so far AI is helping this vendor.</li><li>Against that: the largest fixed commitment is a cloud hosting bill of at least $5.6B over ten years, and the government half of the business is exposed to Washington's calendar.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li>Capex about $30M in nine months; no guide.</li><li>No meaningful buybacks, no dividend, no debt. Cash and Treasuries $10.4B.</li><li>Headcount 4,634. Stock-based pay $318.9M in the quarter.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Alex Karp, co-founder, CEO since 2005.</li><li><strong>Chair:</strong> Peter Thiel, chairman since 2003. The roles are split.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> David Glazer. Start date: not found.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> no; the proxy describes no succession plan.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> three share classes. Class B carries 10 votes; Class F sits in a founder voting trust that lets the founders control up to 49.999999% of the votes. Individual percentages: not found.</li><li><strong>Recent changes or investigations:</strong> a securities class action against officers and directors was dismissed in April 2025 and the appeal was argued in March 2026 (10-Q). Thiel filed to sell 2M shares, about $290M, in March 2026, as reported by Benzinga. On May 20, 2026 Karp sold about $54.1M, Cohen about $43.5M and Sankar about $22.5M, described as tax-related sales on vested stock, as reported by Parameter. Departures: not found.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> a great deal. Karp has run it for two decades, the founders hold close to half the votes whatever they sell, and nobody is named behind him.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"PLTR\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"PLTR · daily · my levels (not yours) chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">PLTR · daily · my levels (not yours)</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"289.0\" y2=\"289.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"204.0\" y2=\"204.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"119.0\" y2=\"119.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"34.0\" y2=\"34.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"10.3\" x2=\"10.3\" y1=\"268.7\" y2=\"276.0\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"8.9\" y=\"272.5\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"2.3\" fill=\"#2F6E4A\" class=\"c-up\"/><line x1=\"14.9\" x2=\"14.9\" y1=\"271.0\" y2=\"299.4\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"277.2\" 